A||N||6||86SEFIERO||how much hp will auto take with v-8||||1||
Z||000000||86SEFIERO||05-30-2000||03:01 AM||||<BR>Im planning on intallng a v-8 into my auto 86se fiero and was wondering if it would take 350hp or is there another auto trans that will work in a fiero v-8 install ? Such as maybe the autotrans off of an oldsmobile 3800 v-6 front wheel drive that they use with the northstar engine.||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||05-30-2000||03:07 PM||||I think the tranny durability is more related to how it's driven and how much torque it has rather than strictly by horsepower. My current engine probably don't put out much more than 210-220 hp, but the torque is probably getting close to 300 ft/lbs. When Shawn runs nitrous on his 2.8, his ponypower is probably getting close to mine, but I bet I still have him beat with torque. Who's tranny will last the longest? Who knows? But, It's a safe bet that the tranny won't last forever if you're leaving long burnt rubber marks on the pavement each time you launch. Using a tranny from different kind of car might work, but it's going to be a lot of work to get it to fit and to work right.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 05-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan86gt3.8||05-30-2000||04:02 PM||||86sefiero, I have seen in kit car a couple times when they did v8's into a finale they used the 4t60e (the tranny they use on 3.8's and the regular cady v8).  I think it was gary zumalt who mated the tranny to the engine but I am not sure.  You might check kit car magazine and see who all has had sbc's mated to the 4t60E(its electronicaly controlled 4speed overdrive)you might be able to do it yourself but the electronics would be a ***** .||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dan86gt3.8||05-30-2000||04:07 PM||||ohhh yeah also anyone selling you a kit will most likely tell you that it depends on how you drive it which is most likely the truth.  And also might look into having the tranny "beefed up" if you are worrying about its reliability.  i think if you go look on archies page it has a link to a site where a guy is running 10's and thats on a "beefed up" slushbox (if I rember correctly) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||05-30-2000||04:41 PM||||The 91-96 3800's use the 4T60E. The 92-96 3800 SC's use the 4T60EHD (heavier final drive, chain and cheese grater. Different valving). <P>In 96, the Series II SC's used the 4T60EHD. <P>In 97 and up, the 4T65E was used on the SC engines. The 4T65E added solenoids to control shift quality and handle a bigger torque converter. They are essentially the same as 4T60EHD's in torque rating.<P>The Northstars use the 4T80E. A TOTALLY DIFFERENT BEAST AND THE SIZE OF MANHATTAN. <P>The 4T60E(HD) can be used, but you would need to have a way to control the shift solenoids. It's possible that a V8 ECM that drives the 4L60E would work, but I've never looked into that. There is little hope of making a 4T65E or 4T80E work cleanly.<P>On a side note, the 4T6X trans are the weakest on a 2-3 shift. That is the typically failure area. If you avoided big sticky tires and learned to launch, it could handle a fair amount of abuse. Mine has at 140k miles. <P>4T60E's can be found in any 3800 SC, 3.4L DOHC, or 3800 U-Van application. The 3800 U-van has the lowest final drive of 3.43:1.||||reg||1
Z||000005||GREENDANO||05-31-2000||07:17 AM||||Another fun fact for the day....440T4's (4T60) were available as an option behind some 2.8's in late 80's Oldsmobiles as well.  So there's the four speed that will handle a little more torque, and the correct bellhousing to bolt up to the 2.8/3.1/3.4  I've been looking around a little to find one for my V8 car.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||3
Z||000006||MikeE||05-31-2000||07:25 AM||||I don't know if I can add anything significant after the wealth of info from TerryK.  BTW, how did you find all that info?  Anyway, due to the heavy traffic in Hawaii and the bad rep of Fiero clutches, I too decided I would rather hve an auto. A year of cross country trucking with a 13sp in my younger days cured me of any love of shifting.  However, I would much rather have a 4sp OD than the stock 3sp.  Instead of trying to mate the trans to a chevy V8, I've thought about searching for a 91 Cadillac, which had a 4.9L V8/4sp OD auto with 200 hp & 275 lb-ft of torque, and swapping in the entire engine/trans/ECM.  That way I would have a modern engine with electronic fuel injection along with the sound and low end torque of a V8 and the benefits of an automatic OD trans (which I believe is a 4T60E).  If I  find a suitable wrecked donor car it would even come at a reasonable price, something that cannot be said about a Northstar or even a 3800 SC.  I know there isn't a large aftermarket for this engine but mild mods like freeing up the exhaust and a K & N filter should bump it a little.  Other normal mods such as porting, extrude honing the intake, having a new chip burned, etc. should also work well.  The info from TerryK about the 3:43 final drive in the vans is enticing since the Cadi's ratio is not that low.  However, the lighter weight of the Fiero may make that moot.  Any thoughts?<P>Aloha, Mike||||reg||6
